NNAAMMEE
       xzdec, lzmadec - Small .xz and .lzma decompressors

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       xxzzddeecc [_o_p_t_i_o_n_._._.]  [_f_i_l_e_._._.]
       llzzmmaaddeecc [_o_p_t_i_o_n_._._.]  [_f_i_l_e_._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       xxzzddeecc is a liblzma-based decompression-only tool for ..xxzz (and only ..xxzz)
       files.  xxzzddeecc is intended to work as a drop-in replacement for xxzz(1) in
       the  most  common  situations where a script has been written to use xxzz
       ----ddeeccoommpprreessss ----ssttddoouutt (and possibly a few other commonly used  options)
       to decompress ..xxzz files.  llzzmmaaddeecc is identical to xxzzddeecc except that llzz‐‐
       mmaaddeecc supports ..llzzmmaa files instead of ..xxzz files.

       To reduce the size of the  executable,  xxzzddeecc  doesn't  support  multi‐
       threading  or  localization,  and doesn't read options from XXZZ__DDEEFFAAUULLTTSS
       and XXZZ__OOPPTT environment variables.  xxzzddeecc doesn't support displaying in‐
       termediate progress information: sending SSIIGGIINNFFOO to xxzzddeecc does nothing,
       but sending  SSIIGGUUSSRR11  terminates  the  process  instead  of  displaying
       progress information.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --dd, ----ddeeccoommpprreessss, ----uunnccoommpprreessss
              Ignored for xxzz(1) compatibility.  xxzzddeecc supports only decompres‐
              sion.

       --kk, ----kkeeeepp
              Ignored for xxzz(1) compatibility.  xxzzddeecc never creates or removes
              any files.

       --cc, ----ssttddoouutt, ----ttoo--ssttddoouutt
              Ignored for xxzz(1) compatibility.  xxzzddeecc always writes the decom‐
              pressed data to standard output.

       --qq, ----qquuiieett
              Specifying this once does nothing since xxzzddeecc never displays any
              warnings or notices.  Specify this twice to suppress errors.

       --QQ, ----nnoo--wwaarrnn
              Ignored for xxzz(1) compatibility.  xxzzddeecc never uses the exit sta‐
              tus 2.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
              Display a help message and exit successfully.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
              Display the version number of xxzzddeecc and liblzma.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       00      All was good.

       11      An error occurred.

       xxzzddeecc doesn't have any warning messages like xxzz(1) has, thus  the  exit
       status 2 is not used by xxzzddeecc.

NNOOTTEESS
       Use  xxzz(1)  instead of xxzzddeecc or llzzmmaaddeecc for normal everyday use.  xxzzddeecc
       or llzzmmaaddeecc are meant only for situations where it is important to  have
       a smaller decompressor than the full-featured xxzz(1).

       xxzzddeecc  and  llzzmmaaddeecc are not really that small.  The size can be reduced
       further by dropping features from liblzma at  compile  time,  but  that
       shouldn't  usually  be done for executables distributed in typical non-
       embedded operating system distributions.  If you need a truly small ..xxzz
       decompressor, consider using XZ Embedded.

